# ── WHY THE PREVIOUS PIN WAS RETIRED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `Qwen/Qwen3.5-122B-A10B` was inherited from ironworks. On 2026-08-27 the provider's catalogue
# (47 models) contained no 122B variant at all, and a turn naming it was rejected:
#   Provider nearai_chat rejected the request: HTTP 400:
#   "Model 'Qwen/Qwen3.5-122B-A10B' not found. It's not a valid model name or alias."
# It had completed turns against this same instance under an hour earlier, so it was withdrawn
# from the catalogue rather than mis-specified. Worth carrying as a lesson: a pin can be
# invalidated by someone else's deployment, with no change on our side and no warning.
#
# ── A MEASUREMENT AND ITS CORRECTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Measured 2026-08-27 (docs/experiments/0001-alloy-drift.md): IronClaw's `usage.cost` bills
# cached and uncached tokens at the SAME rate, so a chained turn reporting 7,360 cached tokens
# cost slightly MORE than an uncached one. That was first read as "caching saves nothing".
#
# It is narrower than that. The provider's own catalogue prices `input_cache_read` at half of
# `input`, so the discount is real on the bill. What does not model it is IronClaw's COMPUTED
# cost field — an extension with its own pricing table. So:
#   * caching IS a genuine saving, at the provider;
#   * `usage.cost` is an estimate that OVERSTATES the cost of a cached turn;
#   * never reconcile spend from `usage.cost` alone.
#
# Re-measured 2026-09-02 at IronClaw 1.4.0, and all three bullets hold — but the cause is not
# what this section assumed, and it is specific to THIS PIN'S NAME
# (docs/experiments/0040-the-cost-field-misses-the-pin-twice.md).
#
# IronClaw's table keys everything off the model string. `qwen/...` trips its `is_local_model`
# name heuristic — a leading `qwen`, like `llama`, `mistral` or `deepseek` — which means "free
# Ollama model", so on CREATE this pin prices at exactly "0". On RETRIEVAL the model has become
# the literal "reborn" (G-2), which the table does not know, so it falls back to its default
# entry and prices the same turn at GPT-4o's $2.50/Mtok in and $10.00/Mtok out. Neither figure
# describes this model. The cache-read divisor is keyed the same way — Anthropic 10x, OpenAI 2x,
# everything else none — so a `qwen` pin gets no discount, which is exactly why the 2026-08-27
# measurement above came out as it did.
#
# So bullet two is confirmed, and the reason is sharper than "an extension with its own pricing
# table": the table would price a KNOWN model correctly on create, and misses this one twice.
# A pin whose name starts with llama/mistral/qwen/deepseek/phi/gemma/yi will read as free.
# Consider that when swapping the pin — not because spend should be read from this field, which
# bullet three forbids, but because a zero there is not evidence of anything.
#
# ── OPEN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The catalogue reports `is_ready: false` for every TEE-attested model including this one. The
# previous pin's comment treated that flag as disqualifying for third-party attested models, yet
# turns against them succeed. Either the flag means something narrower than "usable" or it is
# stale. Resolve before making a privacy claim to anyone outside this repo.
#
# ── A PIN IS NOT IN EFFECT UNTIL A RUNTIME IS TOLD ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Measured 2026-09-07 on the served host: `model-policy check` reported
# `selection_enabled=false, default_matches=false, exact_allowlist=false, allowed_count=0`. The
# runtime had never been told about this pin, so every turn it served answered from IronClaw's own
# default. Nothing was misreported — no document claimed the policy was enforced — but nothing
# measured it either, and the file that calls itself a privacy claim was describing an intention.
# `model-policy apply` corrected it and read the policy back exactly
# (`docs/experiments/0056-the-served-host-was-never-running-the-pinned-model.md`).
#
# **It can drift back and nothing would say so.** `model-policy check` is a manual command on no
# proof path: `dev/prove-host.sh` measures units, confinement, the registry digest and guidance
# hazards, and not this. A runtime rebuilt, restored or replaced comes back with its own default
# and serves. Until that check has a home, treat "the pin is applied" as a fact with a date on it
# rather than a property of the deployment — and re-run `model-policy check` before repeating any
# privacy claim that depends on it.
#
# What applying it establishes is also narrower than the pin's own reasoning above: it constrains
# what may be REQUESTED. The command says so itself — "effective provider model and TEE execution
# are not verified by this check" — and §G-2 records that the runtime reports the model as
# "reborn" on retrieval, so a served turn is not auditable after the fact either.
#
# Context window: 128k. The alloy plus a whole-book envelope is ~29KB (~7k tokens) before the
# runtime's own ~13k-token baseline, so a long chained thread will approach this. That is a
# second, independent reason for the chain-breaking policy in DESIGN.md.
